9
00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:23.409
The grocery platform's notification service.

10
00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:33.076
It carries OTPs, order updates, refunds, slot reminders, price-drop alerts and marketing across push, SMS, WhatsApp, email and in-app.

11
00:00:33.356 --> 00:00:50.188
The chapter covers the classic interview design (fan-out, templates, preferences, queues, retries) plus what production adds: priority under campaigns, exactly-once for the customer on at-least-once plumbing, silently failing vendors, and deciding what not to send.

12
00:00:51.538 --> 00:00:53.223
L1: priority lanes.

13
00:00:53.503 --> 00:00:59.199
A 2.5M-SMS campaign triggered the login and order surge it then competed with.

14
00:00:59.479 --> 00:01:06.178
With one FIFO queue, every OTP expired and order updates had a p99 wait of 82 minutes.

15
00:01:06.458 --> 00:01:17.850
Strict priority protected OTPs but left 130,467 campaign SMS unsent at the 21:00 legal cut-off.

16
00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:22.930
Campaign-friendly fair weights let 11.9% of OTPs expire.

17
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.495
A separate promotional route delivered everything on time.

18
00:01:27.845 --> 00:01:32.005
L2: exactly-once for the customer, on real PostgreSQL.

19
00:01:32.285 --> 00:01:39.432
20,000 events ran through a SKIP LOCKED job table with bus redeliveries, vendor timeouts and worker crashes.

20
00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:45.195
With no dedupe, 1,838 customers got duplicate SMS.

21
00:01:45.475 --> 00:01:48.888
A dedupe key at intake halved that to 884.

22
00:01:49.168 --> 00:01:52.816
Passing an idempotency key the vendor honours took it to 0.

23
00:01:53.096 --> 00:01:54.952
Nothing was lost in any design.

24
00:01:56.302 --> 00:01:58.307
L3: silent vendor failure.

25
00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:06.438
The cheapest vendor delivered only 35% of OTPs for 40 minutes while its API kept returning 200.

26
00:02:06.718 --> 00:02:08.851
Failing over on errors never triggered:

27
00:02:09.131 --> 00:02:13.974
3.9% of OTPs arrived within 30 s during the incident.

28
00:02:14.254 --> 00:02:21.123
Routing on delivery-report health moved traffic 42 s after the failure began and got 90% through.

29
00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:25.649
Adding one resend through another vendor reached 98.9%.

30
00:02:26.999 --> 00:02:28.705
L4: what not to send.

31
00:02:28.985 --> 00:02:42.020
For 100,000 customers over a day, sending everything everywhere cost 50,971 rupeesin SMS and gave 55% of customers more than 10 notifications.

32
00:02:42.300 --> 00:03:02.225
Opt-outs, quiet hours, collapsing, caps, digests and push-first-then-SMS escalation cut SMS spend to 11,067. rupeesCustomers with more than 10 notifications fell to 2.7%, and ~99% of critical updates were still seen within 2 minutes.

33
00:03:02.505 --> 00:03:03.487
Reading time:

34
