35
00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:07.762
Part 1.

36
00:03:08.042 --> 00:03:09.962
Introduction and Why It Matters.

37
00:03:11.212 --> 00:03:12.129
A story:

38
00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:13.476
Diwali night.

39
00:03:13.756 --> 00:03:21.223
At 19:00 on Diwali, marketing launched a 2.5-million-SMS campaign with a big discount.

40
00:03:21.503 --> 00:03:22.377
It worked.

41
00:03:22.657 --> 00:03:28.439
Within minutes, hundreds of thousands of customers opened the app and asked for a login OTP.

42
00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:34.031
The notification service had one queue, one SMS vendor account and one worker pool:

43
00:03:34.311 --> 00:03:36.465
OTPs queued behind the campaign.

44
00:03:36.745 --> 00:03:40.244
They arrived 40 minutes later, long after they had expired.

45
00:03:40.524 --> 00:03:42.977
Customers who wanted to buy couldn't log in.

46
00:03:43.257 --> 00:03:44.772
Order updates queued too.

47
00:03:45.052 --> 00:03:48.295
Your order is out for delivery arrived after the order did.

48
00:03:49.645 --> 00:03:50.626
Duplicates.

49
00:03:50.906 --> 00:03:57.605
A Kafka consumer rebalance during the surge re-delivered thousands of events, and vendor timeouts caused resends.

50
00:03:57.885 --> 00:04:09.085
Some customers got the same OTP three times; some got Your refund of 1,240 rupeesis processed twice and called support asking for the second refund.

51
00:04:09.365 --> 00:04:11.199
The vendor silently degraded.

52
00:04:11.479 --> 00:04:14.829
The cheapest SMS vendor's route to one operator failed.

53
00:04:15.109 --> 00:04:17.946
Its API still returned 200 Accepted.

54
00:04:18.226 --> 00:04:21.853
Nothing alerted until customers complained on social media.

55
00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.626
The campaign missed the window.

56
00:04:23.906 --> 00:04:30.455
The campaign's tail was still sending at 21:40, after the permitted window for promotional SMS.

57
00:04:31.805 --> 00:04:33.320
Notification fatigue.

58
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:40.213
Customers who ordered got six status pushes, five campaign pushes and three price-drop alerts that evening.

59
00:04:40.493 --> 00:04:42.221
Uninstalls rose the next day.

60
00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:57.328
The redesigned service, built in this chapter, treats notifications as a product with obligations: some messages are urgent and cheap to lose, some critical and must arrive once, some optional and must respect consent and time windows.

61
00:04:57.608 --> 00:05:00.019
The architecture follows from those classes.

62
00:05:01.369 --> 00:05:02.051
Quote:

63
00:05:02.331 --> 00:05:03.995
The thesis of this chapter.

64
00:05:04.275 --> 00:05:11.422
A notification system is a fan-out pipeline whose hard problems are not throughput but judgement and guarantees.

65
00:05:11.702 --> 00:05:17.974
Priority: messages differ by orders of magnitude in urgency, and the pipe is shared (L1).

66
00:05:18.254 --> 00:05:28.366
Delivery semantics: every hop is at-least-once, so exactly-once for the customer needs idempotency keys end to end, including at the vendor (L2).

67
00:05:28.646 --> 00:05:35.857
Truth about delivery: vendors fail silently, so route on delivery receipts, not API responses (L3).

68
00:05:36.137 --> 00:05:46.441
Restraint: preferences, quiet hours, collapsing, caps and channel escalation decide what not to send, which is where cost and customer trust are won (L4).

