138
00:08:31.213 --> 00:08:32.087
Part 2.

139
00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:34.245
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

140
00:08:35.495 --> 00:08:39.569
Intuition: a post office with express, registered and bulk mail.

141
00:08:39.849 --> 00:08:57.193
A post office handles express parcels (OTPs: must arrive today, worthless tomorrow), registered letters (refund confirmations: must arrive, exactly once, with proof), and bulk flyers (campaigns: cheap, can wait, must respect no junk mail stickers).

142
00:08:57.473 --> 00:09:01.484
Running them all through one counter means express waits behind flyers.

143
00:09:01.764 --> 00:09:06.628
Sending a registered letter twice because a receipt was lost confuses the recipient.

144
00:09:06.908 --> 00:09:11.708
And the post office must notice when one courier company quietly stops delivering.

145
00:09:13.058 --> 00:09:14.018
Requirements.

146
00:09:14.298 --> 00:09:15.151
Functional.

147
00:09:15.431 --> 00:09:24.754
Accept notification requests from services (API and events), with recipient, template, variables, class, dedupe key and optional schedule.

148
00:09:25.034 --> 00:09:30.389
Render per channel and language from versioned templates (DLT-registered for SMS).

149
00:09:30.669 --> 00:09:35.703
Apply preferences, consent, quiet hours, caps, collapsing and channel selection.

150
00:09:35.983 --> 00:09:43.535
Send through push (FCM or APNs), SMS (multiple vendors), WhatsApp Business, email and in-app inbox.

151
00:09:44.885 --> 00:09:49.067
Track status per message: accepted, sent, delivered, read, failed.

152
00:09:49.347 --> 00:09:51.053
Expose status and history.

153
00:09:51.333 --> 00:09:55.301
Campaigns: audience segments, scheduling, throttling, reporting.

154
00:09:55.581 --> 00:09:56.691
Non-functional.

155
00:09:56.971 --> 00:10:01.088
OTP: p99 10 s end to end, even during campaigns.

156
00:10:01.368 --> 00:10:06.189
Critical: no loss, no customer-visible duplicates; p99 2 min.

157
00:10:06.469 --> 00:10:13.275
Scale: ~5M transactional notifications per day, campaigns up to ~20M recipients.

158
00:10:14.625 --> 00:10:15.691
Availability:

159
00:10:15.971 --> 00:10:20.793
99.95% for intake and urgent or critical sending.

160
00:10:21.073 --> 00:10:27.345
Compliance: consent and opt-out honoured, promotional windows, DLT templates, retention limits.

161
00:10:28.695 --> 00:10:29.740
Estimation.

162
00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:31.364
Here is a quick table:

163
00:10:31.644 --> 00:10:33.073
Quantity, and Estimate.

164
00:10:33.353 --> 00:10:43.487
Transactional: ~5M per day about 60 per second average, ~200 per second evening peak (surges to more during campaigns, L1).

165
00:10:43.767 --> 00:10:50.508
OTP: ~20 per second normal, ~150 per second during a campaign-driven login surge.

166
00:10:50.788 --> 00:10:51.684
Campaigns:

167
00:10:51.964 --> 00:10:59.729
2.5M SMS in 2 h at a 500 per second vendor contract about 83 min of the whole pipe.

168
00:11:00.009 --> 00:11:00.671
Push:

169
00:11:00.951 --> 00:11:07.607
20M campaign pushes via FCM in batches of 500 about 40k API calls.

170
00:11:07.887 --> 00:11:08.783
Storage:

171
00:11:09.063 --> 00:11:17.489
Message log ~1 KB each times ~30M per day about 30 GB per day; keep 90 days hot, summaries longer.

172
00:11:17.769 --> 00:11:18.921
SMS cost:

173
00:11:19.201 --> 00:11:27.436
0.12 rupees, 0.20 per SMS; policy choices change spend by 4 to 5x (L4).

174
00:11:28.786 --> 00:11:31.367
The volume is not large for a modern queue.

175
00:11:31.647 --> 00:11:36.810
What dominates the design is shared vendor capacity, per-message guarantees and cost.

176
00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.824
Architecture of the pipeline.

177
00:11:40.104 --> 00:11:44.392
Intake: an API and a Kafka topic notification.requested.

178
00:11:44.672 --> 00:11:56.064
Each request carries a dedupe key chosen by the producer (for example, ORD-88213:OUTFORDELIVERY), a class, a template id and variables.

179
00:11:56.344 --> 00:12:01.144
Intake validates, stores the request (unique on dedupe key) and acknowledges.

180
00:12:01.424 --> 00:12:09.275
Policy evaluation: load the recipient's preferences, consent, quiet hours, device tokens and recent-send counters.

181
00:12:09.555 --> 00:12:16.211
Decide channels, timing (now, held, digest) and whether this request supersedes a pending one (collapse).

182
00:12:16.491 --> 00:12:20.117
Rendering: template times language times channel, with variables.

183
00:12:20.397 --> 00:12:24.067
SMS templates must match the DLT-registered template.

184
00:12:25.417 --> 00:12:33.545
Channel queues per class: separate queues or lanes for urgent, critical, informational and promotional traffic, per channel (L1).

185
00:12:33.825 --> 00:12:48.267
Channel workers and vendor adapters: pace to each vendor's TPS (Ch 39 egress shaping), pick a vendor by health and cost (L3), send with an idempotency key (L2), and record the vendor message id.

186
00:12:48.547 --> 00:12:54.883
Delivery tracking: vendor webhooks (DLRs), FCM responses, email events, read receipts.

187
00:12:55.163 --> 00:13:01.457
Update message status, feed vendor health, trigger fallbacks (push not seen in 60 s, then SMS).

188
00:13:02.807 --> 00:13:08.311
Inbox and history: in-app inbox and a customer-support view of what was sent and delivered.

189
00:13:08.591 --> 00:13:16.783
Each stage is a separate consumer group so a slow stage doesn't block intake, and each has its own dead-letter handling (Ch 14).

190
00:13:18.133 --> 00:13:19.797
Priority and isolation.

191
00:13:20.077 --> 00:13:38.317
L1 simulated the Diwali evening against a 500 SMS per second vendor contract: a 2.5M-SMS campaign, which then drove OTPs from 20 per second to 150 per second and order updates from 60 per second to 200 per second.

