229
00:15:03.182 --> 00:15:06.851
Never share a FIFO queue between urgent and bulk messages.

230
00:15:07.131 --> 00:15:14.214
Strict priority protects the urgent classes, but bulk traffic gets only what's left, so it can miss legal windows.

231
00:15:14.494 --> 00:15:20.147
Weights tuned for the campaign starve OTPs exactly when the campaign drives logins.

232
00:15:21.497 --> 00:15:26.489
Separate capacity, a promotional vendor account or route, removes the contention.

233
00:15:26.769 --> 00:15:34.748
India's DLT framework already separates promotional from transactional and service messages (different headers and templates).

234
00:15:35.028 --> 00:15:42.900
Size the promotional route so campaigns finish within the permitted window, and schedule campaigns with the surge they create in mind.

235
00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:51.265
The same principle applies to push (separate FCM sending pools per class) and to workers (bulkheads per class, Ch 18).

236
00:15:52.615 --> 00:15:55.730
Delivery semantics: exactly-once for the customer.

237
00:15:56.010 --> 00:16:04.565
Every hop is at-least-once: the event bus redelivers, workers crash, and a vendor call can time out after the vendor accepted the message.

238
00:16:04.845 --> 00:16:10.690
