357
00:22:28.833 --> 00:22:33.419
Consent and opt-outs are obligations, not tuning (Ch 36).

358
00:22:33.699 --> 00:22:39.310
Store them per channel and purpose, honour them at send time, and keep an audit of consent changes.

359
00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:47.803
Time windows: quiet hours for non-urgent messages; legal windows for promotional SMS (TRAI; VERIFY current rules).

360
00:22:48.083 --> 00:22:54.803
Hold messages and send them when the window opens, rather than dropping them, and drop them if they're stale by then.

361
00:22:56.153 --> 00:23:01.615
Collapse superseded updates: packed is pointless if out for delivery follows two minutes later.

362
00:23:01.895 --> 00:23:06.759
Use a collapse key per order; push services also support collapse keys natively.

363
00:23:07.039 --> 00:23:11.199
(L4 counts a collapsed update as seen, because the newer update carries it.).

364
00:23:11.479 --> 00:23:17.964
Frequency caps on promotional messages per user per day and week, and digests for informational alerts.

365
00:23:18.244 --> 00:23:24.943
Channel escalation: push first (free), then SMS only if the push isn't delivered or seen within a deadline.

366
00:23:25.223 --> 00:23:28.785
That's cheaper than sending both, and it's what makes push dependable.

367
00:23:30.135 --> 00:23:32.930
Templates, localisation and India's DLT.

368
00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:41.189
Templates are versioned artefacts with an owner, per channel and language (English, Hindi, Tamil, Kannada,...), with typed variables.

369
00:23:41.469 --> 00:23:45.565
The service renders them; producers send only ids and variables.

370
00:23:45.845 --> 00:23:58.282
DLT (Distributed Ledger Technology registration, TRAI): commercial SMS must use a registered header (sender id) and content template, with variables in registered positions.

371
00:23:58.562 --> 00:24:01.506
Messages that don't match are blocked by operators.

372
00:24:01.786 --> 00:24:08.997
Keep the DLT template id with each template version, and test rendering against the registered pattern in CI.

373
00:24:10.347 --> 00:24:17.984
WhatsApp Business templates are pre-approved by Meta for business-initiated messages outside the 24-hour service window.

374
00:24:18.264 --> 00:24:25.517
Links in templates go through the first-party shortener (Ch 38), which also satisfies URL whitelisting.

375
00:24:25.797 --> 00:24:34.651
Never put secrets or unnecessary personal data in messages beyond what's needed (masked order ids, no full addresses in SMS).

376
00:24:36.001 --> 00:24:37.003
Data model.

377
00:24:37.283 --> 00:24:38.883
Here is the SQL code.

378
00:24:39.163 --> 00:24:42.662
It runs to 16 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

379
00:24:45.512 --> 00:24:55.773
At higher volume, the message log moves to a partitioned table or a wide-column store keyed by user and time, while the working queue stays small (Ch 21).

380
00:24:57.123 --> 00:24:58.297
Campaigns at scale.

381
00:24:58.577 --> 00:25:10.011
Audience resolution runs as a batch job over the customer store (segments, exclusions, opt-outs, caps already used today), producing a recipient list partitioned into chunks.

382
00:25:10.291 --> 00:25:18.419
Throttled release: chunks are released at the promotional route's capacity, and stopped automatically if the platform is degraded (brownout:

383
00:25:18.699 --> 00:25:19.809
Ch 18).

384
00:25:20.089 --> 00:25:24.569
Surge-aware scheduling: a campaign creates logins and orders (L1).

385
00:25:24.849 --> 00:25:28.966
Coordinate with capacity planning, pre-scale, and stagger large sends.

386
00:25:29.246 --> 00:25:32.510
Kill switch per campaign, and a preview or test-send flow.

387
00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:37.355
Measure outcomes (opens, clicks via the shortener, orders), not just sends.

388
00:25:38.705 --> 00:25:40.583
Multi-region and resilience.

389
00:25:40.863 --> 00:25:48.436
Intake in both regions; requests are idempotent by dedupe key, so a replayed request after failover is harmless.

390
00:25:48.716 --> 00:25:55.607
Vendor adapters in both regions, with vendor TPS shared via leased global limits (Ch 39).

391
00:25:55.887 --> 00:25:57.679
If all SMS vendors fail:

392
00:25:57.959 --> 00:26:04.124
OTP falls back to WhatsApp or voice; critical updates to push and in-app; campaigns pause.

393
00:26:04.404 --> 00:26:11.487
Backpressure: if channel queues grow beyond thresholds, shed informational traffic first and hold promotional traffic.

394
00:26:12.837 --> 00:26:13.967
Interview framing.

395
00:26:14.247 --> 00:26:17.810
Interviewers expect fan-out, queues, retries and templates.

396
00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:36.863
Stand out by classifying messages (urgent or critical or informational or promotional) and deriving priority and guarantees from that, by explaining where duplicates come from and how the vendor idempotency key closes the gap, by routing on DLRs rather than API responses,

397
00:26:37.143 --> 00:26:43.373
and by treating restraint (consent, caps, collapse, escalation) as a first-class part of the design.

