11.

702
00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:39.947
Templates hard-coded in services.

703
00:51:40.227 --> 00:51:43.597
Breaks DLT compliance, localisation and ownership.

704
00:51:44.947 --> 00:51:45.865
Watch out:

705
00:51:46.145 --> 00:51:46.934
12.

706
00:51:47.214 --> 00:51:49.838
Campaigns without a kill switch or surge plan.

707
00:51:50.118 --> 00:51:51.974
The campaign becomes the incident.

708
00:51:53.324 --> 00:51:54.199
Part 8.

709
00:51:54.479 --> 00:51:55.823
Interview Q and A.

710
00:51:57.073 --> 00:51:57.905
Beginner.

711
00:51:58.185 --> 00:51:59.209
Question 1.

712
00:51:59.489 --> 00:52:02.945
Design a notification system, what are the main components?

713
00:52:03.225 --> 00:52:04.590
Thirty-second answer:

714
00:52:04.870 --> 00:52:22.342
An intake API or topic where services request notifications with a template id, variables, recipient, class and a dedupe key; a policy engine for preferences, consent, timing and channel choice; a renderer with versioned, localised templates; per-class,

715
00:52:22.622 --> 00:52:35.593
per-channel queues; channel workers with vendor adapters (FCM or APNs, SMS vendors, WhatsApp, email); a delivery tracker consuming receipts; and a message log and in-app inbox.

716
00:52:36.943 --> 00:52:37.945
Question 2.

717
00:52:38.225 --> 00:52:40.871
Why separate queues for OTPs and marketing?

718
00:52:41.151 --> 00:52:42.516
Thirty-second answer:

719
00:52:42.796 --> 00:52:46.529
They differ by orders of magnitude in urgency and volume.

720
00:52:46.809 --> 00:52:52.463
In a shared FIFO queue, OTPs wait behind millions of campaign messages and expire.

721
00:52:52.743 --> 00:53:00.252
Separate lanes with priority (and ideally a separate promotional route) keep OTPs at seconds while campaigns drain.

722
00:53:01.602 --> 00:53:02.647
Question 3.

723
00:53:02.927 --> 00:53:05.146
What is a DLR and why does it matter?

724
00:53:05.426 --> 00:53:06.791
Thirty-second answer:

725
00:53:07.071 --> 00:53:13.663
A delivery report: the vendor's asynchronous callback saying whether an SMS reached the handset (or failed).

726
00:53:13.943 --> 00:53:17.591
The send API only says the vendor accepted the message.

727
00:53:17.871 --> 00:53:24.357
DLRs are the only real signal of delivery, so they drive status, retries, vendor health and routing.

728
00:53:25.707 --> 00:53:26.773
Question 4.

729
00:53:27.053 --> 00:53:29.805
How do you handle user preferences and opt-outs?

730
00:53:30.085 --> 00:53:31.451
Thirty-second answer:

731
00:53:31.731 --> 00:53:46.728
Store consent per user, channel and purpose (transactional versus promotional), with timestamps and source; evaluate at send time (not at campaign creation); honour opt-out keywords and in-app settings immediately; keep an audit trail.

732
00:53:47.008 --> 00:53:51.381
Transactional service messages follow different rules from promotional ones.

733
00:53:52.731 --> 00:53:53.734
Intermediate.

734
00:53:54.014 --> 00:53:55.123
Question 5.

735
00:53:55.403 --> 00:53:58.326
How do you avoid sending duplicate notifications?

736
00:53:58.606 --> 00:53:59.971
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:54:00.251 --> 00:54:08.657
Producers supply a dedupe key per logical notification; intake stores it with a unique constraint so redelivered events are dropped.

738
00:54:08.937 --> 00:54:12.243
Workers claim jobs with leases and retry on failure.

739
00:54:12.523 --> 00:54:19.094
For unknown outcomes (timeouts, crashes after send), pass an idempotency key the vendor dedupes on.

740
00:54:19.374 --> 00:54:24.750
Where impossible, pick duplicate-versus-loss per class and word messages idempotently.

741
00:54:26.100 --> 00:54:27.209
Question 6.

742
00:54:27.489 --> 00:54:30.647
How do workers claim jobs safely from a database queue?

743
00:54:30.927 --> 00:54:32.292
Thirty-second answer:

744
00:54:32.572 --> 00:54:33.425
UPDATE...

745
00:54:33.705 --> 00:54:35.113
WHERE id IN (SELECT...

746
00:54:35.393 --> 00:54:41.132
FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT n) RETURNING... setting a lease expiry and incrementing attempts.

747
00:54:41.412 --> 00:54:43.332
Other workers skip locked rows.

748
00:54:43.612 --> 00:54:47.409
If a worker dies, its lease expires and the job is reclaimed.

749
00:54:47.689 --> 00:54:52.660
Finished jobs are marked done; failures get a backoff and eventually a dead-letter state.

750
00:54:54.010 --> 00:54:55.098
Question 7.

751
00:54:55.378 --> 00:54:58.855
Push, SMS, WhatsApp or email, how do you choose the channel?

752
00:54:59.135 --> 00:55:00.501
Thirty-second answer:

753
00:55:00.781 --> 00:55:04.258
By message class, user preference, reachability and cost.

754
00:55:04.538 --> 00:55:17.573
Push is free but unreliable (tokens, OEM throttling); SMS is reliable and paid; WhatsApp is rich and cheaper for some templates but needs opt-in; email suits receipts and digests.

755
00:55:17.853 --> 00:55:23.549
For critical messages use push first and escalate to SMS if not seen within a deadline.

756
00:55:24.899 --> 00:55:25.923
Question 8.

757
00:55:26.203 --> 00:55:28.037
What is notification collapsing?

758
00:55:28.317 --> 00:55:29.683
Thirty-second answer:

759
00:55:29.963 --> 00:55:39.520
Replacing pending or recent notifications that a newer one supersedes, packed followed by out for delivery two minutes later becomes one notification.

760
00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:47.672
Implement with a collapse key per entity, short holding windows for fast-changing states, and the push services' native collapse keys.

761
00:55:49.022 --> 00:55:50.089
Question 9.

762
00:55:50.369 --> 00:55:53.782
How do you template messages for many languages and channels?

763
00:55:54.062 --> 00:55:55.427
Thirty-second answer:

764
00:55:55.707 --> 00:56:05.371
A template registry: id, versions, channel variants, languages, typed variables, owner, and for SMS the DLT header and template id.

765
00:56:05.651 --> 00:56:12.755
Producers send template id + variables; the renderer chooses language from preferences and validates variables.

766
00:56:13.035 --> 00:56:15.979
CI tests rendering against DLT patterns.

767
00:56:17.329 --> 00:56:18.097
Senior.

768
00:56:18.377 --> 00:56:19.423
Question 10.

769
00:56:19.703 --> 00:56:24.183
An SMS vendor is returning 200 but messages aren't arriving.

770
00:56:24.463 --> 00:56:26.511
How does your system detect and react?

771
00:56:26.791 --> 00:56:28.156
Thirty-second answer:

772
00:56:28.436 --> 00:56:37.268
Track DLR-based delivery rate per vendor (and per operator or circle) over a sliding window, judging each send after its deadline.

773
00:56:37.548 --> 00:56:49.964
When it drops below a threshold, route to the next-best healthy vendor, keep a small probe share on the sick one to detect recovery, alert, and resend recent undelivered OTPs through another vendor.

774
00:56:51.314 --> 00:56:52.445
Question 11.

775
00:56:52.725 --> 00:56:57.439
How do you run a 20M-recipient campaign without hurting transactional traffic?

776
00:56:57.719 --> 00:56:59.085
Thirty-second answer:

777
00:56:59.365 --> 00:57:16.559
Separate promotional lane, workers and vendor route; resolve the audience in batch with opt-outs and caps; release in throttled chunks within the legal window; coordinate with capacity for the surge the campaign creates; brownout hooks to pause; kill switch; measure outcomes.

778
00:57:17.909 --> 00:57:18.997
Question 12.

779
00:57:19.277 --> 00:57:21.624
Exactly-once delivery, is it possible?

780
00:57:21.904 --> 00:57:23.269
Thirty-second answer:

781
00:57:23.549 --> 00:57:28.264
Not end to end with an external vendor unless the vendor deduplicates on your key.

782
00:57:28.544 --> 00:57:39.381
You can make everything up to the vendor call effectively exactly-once (dedupe keys, transactional state), and with vendor idempotency the customer sees exactly one message.

783
00:57:39.661 --> 00:57:45.891
Otherwise you choose at-least-once with idempotent content, or at-most-once and accept rare losses.

784
00:57:47.241 --> 00:57:48.521
Question 13.

785
00:57:48.801 --> 00:57:52.257
How would you implement quiet hours and legal time windows?

786
00:57:52.537 --> 00:57:53.902
Thirty-second answer:

787
00:57:54.182 --> 00:58:06.662
Evaluate at send time in the user's time zone: non-urgent messages outside the window go to a hold queue keyed by release time; at release, re-check freshness (drop if stale) and consent.

788
00:58:06.942 --> 00:58:11.977
Promotional SMS respect regulatory windows regardless of preferences.

789
00:58:12.257 --> 00:58:15.627
Urgent and critical messages bypass quiet hours.

790
00:58:16.977 --> 00:58:18.172
Question 14.

791
00:58:18.452 --> 00:58:22.100
How do you design retries and dead letters for notifications?

792
00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:23.745
Thirty-second answer:

793
00:58:24.025 --> 00:58:46.959
Retry transient failures (503, timeouts) with exponential backoff and jitter, bounded by a per-class deadline (OTPs are useless after 5 min, so don't retry past it); switch vendor on repeated failure; after max attempts or deadline, move to a dead-letter state with reason, alert on DLQ growth, and allow replay for critical classes.

794
00:58:48.309 --> 00:58:49.205
Architect.

795
00:58:49.485 --> 00:58:50.679
Question 15.

796
00:58:50.959 --> 00:58:55.397
Design the notification platform for a national grocery app end to end.

797
00:58:55.677 --> 00:58:57.042
Thirty-second answer:

798
00:58:57.322 --> 00:59:18.591
Message classes with lanes and routes; intake with dedupe keys; a policy engine for consent, windows, caps, collapse and escalation; a template registry with DLT ids; channel workers with leases and vendor idempotency; multi-vendor routing on DLR health with OTP resends; a delivery tracker,

799
00:59:18.871 --> 00:59:26.935
message log and inbox; campaigns with throttled release; observability on latency per class, DLR rates and spend.

800
00:59:28.285 --> 00:59:29.480
Question 16.

801
00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:32.832
Build or buy, would you use a notification SaaS?

802
00:59:33.112 --> 00:59:34.477
Thirty-second answer:

803
00:59:34.757 --> 00:59:56.475
Buy the channels (FCM, SMS or WhatsApp aggregators, email providers) and possibly an orchestration SaaS for marketing journeys; build or tightly control the transactional core, dedupe, priority, routing on DLRs, consent and DLT compliance, because it's on the critical path for login and orders and needs your data.

804
00:59:56.755 --> 00:59:58.547
Keep vendor adapters pluggable.

805
00:59:59.897 --> 01:00:01.241
Question 17.

806
01:00:01.521 --> 01:00:04.145
How do you measure the notification system's success?

807
01:00:04.425 --> 01:00:05.790
Thirty-second answer:

808
01:00:06.070 --> 01:00:25.547
Per class: delivery latency and success (OTP within 30 s, critical within 2 min), duplicate and loss rates, spend per delivered useful message; for engagement: opens, clicks, conversions, opt-out and uninstall rates, notifications per user; for compliance: violations (should be zero).

809
01:00:26.897 --> 01:00:28.071
Question 18.

810
01:00:28.351 --> 01:00:34.303
How does the notification platform interact with the rest of the architecture during a regional outage?

811
01:00:34.583 --> 01:00:35.948
Thirty-second answer:

812
01:00:36.228 --> 01:00:56.473
Intake in both regions with idempotent dedupe keys; queues replicated or re-driven from the event log; vendor adapters in both regions with shared TPS via leased limits; OTP falls back across vendors and channels; promotional traffic pauses automatically; critical backlog drains first on recovery.

813
01:00:57.823 --> 01:00:59.061
Question 19.

814
01:00:59.341 --> 01:01:02.605
How do you govern notifications across many product teams?

815
01:01:02.885 --> 01:01:04.250
Thirty-second answer:

816
01:01:04.530 --> 01:01:22.215
A platform with a policy layer every team must go through: shared frequency caps and priorities per user across teams, template ownership and review, class definitions, budgets per team, dashboards for sends and opt-outs by team, and an approval path for new promotional types.

817
01:01:22.495 --> 01:01:26.506
Teams keep autonomy over content and triggers within those guardrails.

818
01:01:27.856 --> 01:01:28.965
Question 20.

819
01:01:29.245 --> 01:01:32.189
What privacy and compliance obligations apply?

820
01:01:32.469 --> 01:01:33.835
Thirty-second answer:

821
01:01:34.115 --> 01:01:53.464
Consent per purpose and channel with audit; opt-out honoured promptly; DLT registration of headers and templates; promotional time windows; minimising personal data in message content; retention limits on message logs and erasure support; vendor contracts covering data handling and residency.

822
01:01:54.814 --> 01:01:55.710
Part 9.

823
01:01:55.990 --> 01:01:57.398
Summary and Practice.

824
01:01:58.648 --> 01:01:59.736
Key takeaways.

825
01:02:00.016 --> 01:02:07.909
Classify messages (urgent, critical, informational, promotional); priority and guarantees follow (§1.2).

826
01:02:08.189 --> 01:02:15.208
Isolate capacity: lanes and a separate promotional route; campaigns create the surge they compete with (L1).

827
01:02:15.488 --> 01:02:21.333
Exactly-once for the customer needs dedupe keys, leases and vendor idempotency (L2).

828
01:02:21.613 --> 01:02:27.821
Route on DLRs, not API responses; resend OTPs through another vendor (L3).

829
01:02:29.171 --> 01:02:35.849
Restraint is design: consent, windows, collapse, caps, digests and push-first escalation (L4).

830
01:02:36.129 --> 01:02:40.310
Templates are governed artefacts with DLT ids and languages.

831
01:02:40.590 --> 01:02:43.406
Measure useful delivery and fatigue, not sends.

832
01:02:44.756 --> 01:02:46.079
Decision cheat sheet.

833
01:02:46.359 --> 01:02:47.703
Here is a quick table:

834
01:02:47.983 --> 01:02:49.156
Need, and Choice.

835
01:02:49.436 --> 01:02:50.097
OTP:

836
01:02:50.377 --> 01:02:57.161
SMS strict priority lane, DLR routing, resend via alternate vendor, WhatsApp or voice fallback.

837
01:02:57.441 --> 01:02:58.593
Critical updates:

838
01:02:58.873 --> 01:03:03.673
Push first, SMS at 60 s if unseen, collapse keys, dedupe keys.

839
01:03:03.953 --> 01:03:04.999
Informational:

840
01:03:05.279 --> 01:03:07.199
Push or in-app, daily digest.

841
01:03:07.479 --> 01:03:08.396
Promotional:

842
01:03:08.676 --> 01:03:12.793
Separate route, consent at send time, caps, windows, throttled release.

843
01:03:13.073 --> 01:03:13.927
Job queue:

844
01:03:14.207 --> 01:03:18.537
Kafka per class, or PostgreSQL SKIP LOCKED at moderate volume.

845
01:03:18.817 --> 01:03:19.777
Duplicates:

846
01:03:20.057 --> 01:03:24.687
Dedupe key at intake + vendor client reference + idempotent wording.

847
01:03:26.037 --> 01:03:27.167
Exercises.

848
01:03:27.447 --> 01:03:34.295
(B2) In L1, add a WhatsApp route for OTPs that absorbs 30% of OTPs during the surge.

849
01:03:34.575 --> 01:03:37.135
How much capacity does that free for the campaign?

850
01:03:37.415 --> 01:03:43.389
(B3) In L2, shorten the lease to 50 ms and add a 100 ms vendor latency.

851
01:03:43.669 --> 01:03:45.738
What new source of duplicates appears?

852
01:03:46.018 --> 01:03:53.079
(B3) In L3, make the failure operator-specific (40% of numbers) and compute health per operator.

853
01:03:53.359 --> 01:03:55.023
Compare with vendor-level health.

854
01:03:55.303 --> 01:04:02.429
(B4) In L4, add a shared cap of 6 notifications per user per day across all classes except critical.

855
01:04:02.709 --> 01:04:04.415
What happens to marketing reach?

856
01:04:05.765 --> 01:04:16.603
(B4) Design the schema and API for a customer-support view that shows every message sent to a customer, with delivery status, without exposing OTP values.

857
01:04:17.953 --> 01:04:18.955
